From: Andrei Kholodnyi <andrei.kholodnyi@windriver.com>
Date: Fri, 20 Nov 2026 17:00:00 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] Fast-DDS VxWorks: robust locks never reopen a lock file this process holds

VxWorks has no flock(); unixextra emulates it with fcntl() record locks, which
belong to the process, and the kernel drops all of them on a file as soon as
the process closes ANY descriptor of it (POSIX fcntl() semantics). The SHM
transport's RobustExclusiveLock / RobustSharedLock open a second descriptor
for Port::is_zombie() and is_locked() and close it again. On a lock file the
process holds itself that
  - answered wrongly: the process' own lock does not conflict, so it judged
    its own live port a zombie (exclusive and shared), and
  - dropped the real lock on close, so a peer then saw the port unlocked.
(0002 fixed one instance of this, stat() on the path, with fstat().)

Add RobustLockRegistry, the lock files this process holds (path -> descriptor,
holders, exclusive/shared), and answer from it: acquiring an exclusive lock the
process holds throws (as with BSD flock()), a second shared lock joins the
existing holder (same descriptor; closed with the last holder), is_locked() /
test_lock() report LOCKED, all without opening the file. Acquire and release
run under the registry lock. Guarded by __VXWORKS__.

Verified on QEMU (zombie test, holder runs both is_zombie() checks on its own
ports, another process probes the locks): stock headers judge both own ports
zombie and lose both locks; patched: not zombie, locks held, released after
the holders go.
